pdated using new velocities. If we process each i independently, we can compute force using the original positions array (read-only), update velocity in a separate velocity buffer, and then compute new position into another position buffer. Then after all bodies processed, swap buffers for next iteration.\n\nSo we need:\n- pos arrays: x_cur, y_cur, z_cur (current positions)\n- vel arrays: vx_cur, vy_cur, vz_cur (current velocities) - but velocity update uses forces from current positions; then position update uses new velocities. We can have two sets of velocity buffers? Actually we can compute new velocity into a separate buffer and then use it to update position into another buffer. Or we can do in-place for velocity after force computation because each i is independent (force only reads positions, not velocities). So we can read current positions, compute forces, update vx[i] += dt*fx etc., then compute new x = old_x + new_vx*dt and store into next position buffer. Then swap position buffers.\n\nSo per iteration:\n- For each i in parallel: \n   - Read xi,yi,zi from cur_pos arrays.\n   - Compute forces by iterating j over all bodies using cur_pos arrays (read-only).\n   - Update vx[i], vy[i], vz[i] in place (since no other thread touches same i).\n   - Write new positions into next_pos arrays: nx[i] = xi + vx[i]*DT, etc.\n- After loop, swap pointers for pos arrays.\n\nThis fuses force and integration. We need to preserve exact per-body summation order; unrolling by 4 with sequential accumulation does that.\n\nWe also want a single OpenMP parallel region spanning all iterations to avoid thread creation overhead. So we put #pragma omp parallel around the whole iteration loop, and inside use #pragma omp for simd? Actually we need worksharing across i. We can have:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n  float *x = x_cur; ... // but pointers are shared; each thread needs to access arrays.\n  for (int iter=0; iter<N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (int i=0; i<nbodies; ++i) { compute force and update }\n    // after loop, swap pointers. But pointer swapping must be done by one thread only.\n  }\n}\n\nBut if we have a parallel region, the iteration loop is executed by all threads redundantly? Actually each thread executes the same code unless worksharing directives distribute loops. We want to keep the outer iteration sequential (each iteration depends on previous), but inside each iteration we parallelize over i. So we can put #pragma omp for inside the parallel region, and after that loop we need a barrier before swapping pointers? Actually all threads must finish their assigned i's before any thread swaps pointers because next iteration reads from new positions. We can use implicit barrier at end of worksharing construct (omp for has implicit barrier unless nowait). So after #pragma omp for, there is an implicit barrier; then we need to swap pointers only once. We can have one thread do the swap using a critical or master region. But pointer swapping is just changing local variables? Actually x_cur etc are shared variables in main scope. If we declare them as shared and modify inside parallel region, all threads see same variable but modifications must be synchronized. Better to use two sets of arrays and alternate indices: e.g., have pos[2][n] arrays and an index cur=0; after each iteration, cur = 1-cur. That avoids pointer swapping races. We can allocate double buffers for positions only (since velocities are updated in place). So we need x[2][nbodies], y[2][nbodies], z[2][nbodies]. Velocities single arrays vx,vy,vz.\n\nThen inside parallel region:\nint cur = 0;\nfor iter...\n   #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n   for i: compute using pos[cur] arrays; update velocities; write new positions into pos[1-cur].\n   // implicit barrier\n   cur = 1 - cur; // but this must be done by all threads? Actually if we do it after the loop, each thread would execute it and they might not agree. We need to ensure only one